# Port patch, wish of the project owner (07.11.2026): the app's picture format "Native", the screen's own aspect, on
# the widescreen of Noname267's fork (19.5:9, branch Noname267-patch-1, its 0210-widescreen-aspect.patch; not taken
# over as it was). The game computes its 3D for 4:3: C_MTXPerspective with 4/3 in camera.cpp, the culling frustum
# VIEW_ASPECT in view.cpp, and everything that projects with the camera's fovy at 4/3 (the effects that lay a copy of
# the picture over the scene: esp08, esp0f, esp18, esp_sub, Espgen42, espgen45, id_sys, trans; the mirrors' render
# texture TexRender and mirror; the item camera of examine; the camera's near plane against walls, cam_qfps, and water,
# cam_ctrl; the ray through a screen point, CamPos2ScrnVec). The app shows the rows 56..392 of the picture, the 16:9
# inside the game's letterbox, over a screen wider than 16:9; with "Native" on and while the player plays,
# re4_view_aspect (runtime/picture.cpp) gives 4/3 x (the screen's aspect) / (16/9) for the camera's projection (19.5:9:
# 1.625) and re4_view_camera_aspect the aspect of the projection pG->Camera has to the others, so the game draws and
# culls the wider view. The id system's HUD is drawn in front of the camera with its projection and keeps its 16:9
# shape in the middle; the message text is drawn flat (messageCamera, 512 x 384) and gets the same narrowing
# (re4_view_flat_edge). CameraSetProjection tells the GX layer which projection a picture loads first (re4_view_load,
# RE4_GX_CHUNK_WIDE): the app shows a wide picture over the whole screen, any other in the 16:9 box. Sub screens,
# the memory card and option screens, the result tables, the rifle's scope and movies stay 4:3 (shown in the 16:9 box,
# as "16:9"). Every replacement is on its line (the files keep their line numbers); the functions are declared in
# re4_whole_prelude.h. Off, and in every other picture format, each gives the original's 4:3 constant (1.3333334f;
# 0 and 512 for the text), so nothing changes. A port feature, not a fix of the prototype.
